/**
@page cosmology_parallel MPI, execution spaces and memory movement

@section parallel_collective Collective operations

Construction of @ref cosmology::Simulation, field FFTs, particle migration,
particle-count checks, reductions and simulation entry points involve all ranks
of the active IPPL communicator. Every rank must call them in the same order
with a consistent configuration. Host scalar quadratures are replicated per
rank and are not device callbacks. Scalar sums/maxima use MPI_Allreduce;
particle counts use MPI_UINT64_T, not floating-point accumulation.

Root performs setup/diagnostic filesystem operations; snapshots are per-rank
files. Diagnostic adapters explicitly broadcast root input errors before
scatter. The normal application catches fatal exceptions and calls MPI_Abort;
catching an error on only one rank and continuing would strand other ranks in
a collective. The class is not a thread-safe API for unrelated concurrent runs
sharing the same communicator.

@section parallel_device Execution and storage

Production particle attributes and distributed fields use Kokkos's default
execution/memory spaces. CIC atomics, lattice construction, coefficient
creation, wrapping, force construction and integration run there. Growth,
transfer reading, adaptive quadrature, filesystem I/O and metadata formatting
run on the host. Mathematical calls in device kernels use Kokkos functions;
host numerical routines use standard-library functions.

Gaussian initialization builds a host radial P(k) table indexed by integer
squared mode length (at most 3(N/2)^2), then deep-copies it once to execution
memory. This replicates O(N^2) data on each rank; it is not an exascale-memory
claim. Snapshot output creates host mirrors and explicitly deep-copies IDs,
positions and momentum before serialization. Python analysis uses host NumPy
arrays/DataFrames; its FFTs and archives do not imply device execution.

@section parallel_reproducibility Reproducibility and limits

Global Fourier keys make the production Gaussian mode assignment independent
of rank ownership at fixed N. MPI reduction trees, CIC atomics, FFT backends and
floating-point execution ordering can change final rounding. Reproducibility
means agreement under the stated comparisons, not universal bitwise identity.
Native FastPM precision and Nyquist behavior are retained rather than patched
to force agreement. Common-phase Python fixtures have their own explicit
physical-mode and once-rounded momentum contract.

`threads` in historical metadata means DefaultExecutionSpace.concurrency();
on CUDA this is not a count of host OpenMP threads or resident GPU threads.
New metadata also records `execution_concurrency`, `host_threads`,
`execution_space` and `memory_space`. Neither GPU metadata nor a launcher smoke
check establishes GPU physics correctness. The default local campaign uses one
host OpenMP thread per MPI rank. It is a physics comparison, not a strong-scaling
benchmark. Kokkos and heFFTe are external dependencies @cite trott2022kokkos
@cite ayala2020heffte; their citations do not qualify untested hardware.

@page cosmology_validation Validation ladder and interpretation

@section validation_ladder Properties established by each layer

| Layer | Entry point | What it checks | What it does not establish |
|---|---|---|---|
| Host model | TestCosmologyPhysics | Parser restrictions, independent growth ODE, EdS factors, quadrature, transfer/normalization | Distributed particle forces |
| Spectral/migration | Cosmology --self-test; ranks 1–4 | Manufactured field force, real output, multi-box wrapping, unique IDs, migrated attributes | Nonlinear halo accuracy |
| Linear evolution | validate_linear.py | Uniform zero force, Gaussian/sine ICs and resolved linear growth, mesh/time/rank controls | General shell crossing |
| Frozen native operators | validate_frozen_force.py | Each full CIC/FFT/gather operator against its own oracle; predicted cross-code residual | Identical raw Nyquist operators |
| Planar analytical evolution | validate_pancake.py | Continuum Zel'dovich trajectories before crossing; temporal/spatial controls | An analytical post-crossing truth |
| Imported-state regression | test_frozen_adapter.py, test_evolution_adapter.py | Malformed inputs, exact IDs/counts, state imports, ballistic/oracle paths, production-driver equivalence | Production restart robustness |
| Matched native PM | validate_evolution.py | Common particles, backgrounds, force/integration schedules, resolved observables and refinements | Halo/statistical universality |
| Crossed resolution | validate_resolution_study.py | Independent particle/mesh controls, translations, timestep/start/rank effects and complete qualification prefixes | A pass inferred from cross-code agreement alone |
| Three-code A11 | run_three_code_campaign.py | Same local IC, complete z=0 outputs and common spectra for IPPL/FastPM/TreePM | Continuum accuracy or identical force models |
| Saved completion audits | audit_cpu_study.py, audit_a100_study.py | Expected scope, artifacts, hashes and recorded check identities | A new scientific pass |

@section validation_metrics Conservation, stability and error budgets

Global particle count is exact integer bookkeeping. CIC deposition is expected
to conserve unit weights, but its floating reduction is not an exact integer
sum. The production force path rejects a nonfinite relative mass residual or
one above 10^-10. External campaign budgets may be tighter and remain separate;
passing the production check does not erase an external failure. Net force,
mean momentum, symmetry and inverse imaginary residuals are sanity observables,
not interchangeable proofs of accuracy.

Timestep orders use successive differences above declared floors; linear
spatial growth is measured on resolved wavelengths. Smaller global RMS does not
by itself establish local Jacobian/peak-density accuracy. A negative sampled
initial-map eigenvalue is rejected; it is not repaired by resampling or fitted
amplitude scaling. A planar analytic map ceases to be a reference after shell
crossing. Cosmological expansion does not conserve ordinary mechanical energy.

All thresholds belong to the responsible validator and are frozen before a
campaign. Historical failures are retained in their reports. Successful
execution, intact archives, and agreement between two discretizations can all
coexist with failure of a resolution qualification. A band of qualified modes
requires every declared control; a retrospective favorable subset is not a
replacement qualification.

@section validation_archive Numerical versus byte-preserving archives

The matched-evolution gzip archives preserve original CSV bytes, verified by
compressed and decompressed hashes. Disk-aware study NPZ archives instead
preserve uint64 IDs and float64 phase-space arrays with canonical digests,
including signed zeros; they do not reconstruct original CSV formatting.
Qualification is the caller's responsibility, not a side effect of archival
completion. Provenance includes binaries, relevant source, initial data,
schedules, logs and output checks; no script should manufacture missing evidence.

@section validation_run Running checks

@code{.sh}
ctest --test-dir build_openmp -L cosmology --output-on-failure
/Users/adelmann/.venv-h6/bin/python -B -m unittest discover \
  -s demos/cosmology/python/tests -p 'test_*.py'
@endcode

Native reference downloads/builds are opt-in and never part of normal CMake
configuration or CTest. See @ref cosmology_tools for exact executable/script
arguments, the separate quick/full workflows and machine-specific launchers.
*/
